#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054494 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67e6a951-2645-5440-8059-14f0182933ff" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Few Terms for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/id #uuid "3c7dd3ad-eaa9-5312-adaf-3a0772e845f2" :question/math-academy-id "e-762" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=1$ is given by\n$\\displaystyle f(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty {{{n}}(x-1)^{n}}$.\nCalculate the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor series expansion about $x=1$ of $f'(x)$." :question/worked-solution "Writing the first few terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty {{{n}}(x-1)^{n}} = (x-1) + {2(x-1)^2} + {3(x-1)^3} +\\cdots\n$$\n\nTo find $f'(x)$, we differentiate term-by-term. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= 1 + 0 + 4(x - 1) + 9(x - 1)^{2} + ⋯ \\\\\n&= 1 + 4(x - 1) + 9(x - 1)^{2} + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054495 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d418552d-d147-5736-8acd-5ba237a90e07" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the a General Term for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/id #uuid "cb7a106a-130a-5479-b60d-8ea76a8cd8de" :question/math-academy-id "e-763" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=3$ is given by\n$\\displaystyle f(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(x-3)^{n}}{n}$\nFind $a_n$, given that\n\n$f'(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n$." :question/worked-solution "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\nn\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n$n$\nWe can now write the Taylor series for $f'(x)$ using the sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty (x-3)^{n-1}\n$$\n\nComparing the series above with the given series, we can deduce that\n\n$$\na_n = (x-3)^{n-1}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054496 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88b9751d-450e-5ae9-bda6-1c1d136bc929" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Convergence for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/id #uuid "e656ee8d-55b9-5ea6-beae-7c2caf329a6a" :question/math-academy-id "e-764" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n$\\displaystyle f(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-3)^{n}\\,x^n}{n^3}$\nThe series converges to $f(x)$ for $\\mid x \\mid < R$, where $R$ is the radius of convergence of the Taylor series. Calculate the radius of convergence of the series expansion of $f'(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)$, about $x=0$." :question/worked-solution "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of $f'(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x)$. So, to find the radius of convergence of $f'(x)$, we need to compute $R$.\n\nTo calculate $R$, we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= lim_(n → ∞) \\mid \\frac{a_{n + 1}}{a_{n}} \\mid \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞) \\mid ((-3)^{n + 1}x^{n + 1})/((n + 1)^{3}) \\cdot (n^{3})/((-3)^{n}x^{n}) \\mid \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞) \\mid ((-3)x \\cdot n^{3})/((n + 1)^{3}) \\mid \\\\\n&= 3\\begin{vmatrix}x & lim_(n → ∞)(1)/((1 + \\frac{1}{n})^{3}) \\\\ = 3 & x\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges when $L<1$. So, it converges when\n\n$$\n3 \\mid x \\mid \\mid < 1; \\mid x \\mid \\mid < \\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of convergence is\n\n$$\nR = \\dfrac 1 3\n$$"}]}
